          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is there a link between sleep and migraine?",
          "answer": "Sleep and migraine have a two-way relationship, meaning they can both affect each other. Disturbed sleep can make migraine attacks more likely, and conversely, migraine attacks can disturb sleep. Sleep is also noted as one of the ways a migraine attack can end.\n\nA change in sleep pattern is a commonly reported trigger for migraines. It is important to note that regularity in sleep is often more impactful than the total duration of sleep. Both getting too little sleep and sleeping in can trigger migraine attacks. This is why weekend headaches are a common pattern, as changes in sleep timing over the weekend can provoke them.\n\nThis information comes from the \"General clinical reference and headache medicine educational summary\" document, specifically the section \"Sleep and migraine: regularity, and the relationship running both ways.\"\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
